{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>Persistent Queue</h1>
    <p>
        The queue runs work <strong>in the background, outside the web request</strong>, and keeps it safe until it has run. Jobs are stored in your database, so they survive a server restart, are retried when they fail, and can be processed by one worker or many.
    </p>

    <pre><code class="language-php">// In a controller: queue the job and answer the visitor straight away
dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($user->id));

return $this->view('auth/registered.twig');</code></pre>

    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4 my-4">
        <strong>Queue or <code>defer()</code>?</strong> <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/defer"><code>defer()</code></a> runs a task right after the response, in the same process, and forgets it if the server stops. The queue stores the job first. Use <code>defer()</code> for quick best-effort work; use the queue for anything that must not be lost (payments, important emails, imports) or that needs retries.
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Quick Start</h2>
    <p><strong>1. Create the tables</strong> (once per project):</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody queue:install --migrate</code></pre>
    <p>This publishes a migration into <code>db/migrations</code> and runs it. Without <code>--migrate</code> it only publishes the file; run <code>php rhapsody migrate</code> yourself when you are ready.</p>

    <p><strong>2. Write a job:</strong></p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">namespace App\Jobs;

use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\JobInterface;

final class SendWelcomeEmail implements JobInterface
{
    public function __construct(public readonly int $userId) {}

    public function handle(UserRepository $users, WelcomeMailer $mailer): void
    {
        $mailer->sendTo($users->find($this->userId));
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>3. Dispatch it:</strong></p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($user->id));</code></pre>

    <p><strong>4. Run a worker:</strong></p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody queue:work</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Writing Jobs</h2>
    <p>A job is a small class that implements <code>JobInterface</code> and has a public <code>handle()</code> method.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>handle()</code> gets its dependencies from the container.</strong> Type-hint the services you need, exactly as you would in a controller. The worker calls it through <code>Container::call()</code>, so aliases, singletons and closure bindings all apply.</li>
        <li><strong>The constructor holds what you dispatch with.</strong> Pass plain values: an id, a string, a number. Look the full record up inside <code>handle()</code> so the job always works with fresh data.</li>
        <li><strong>No closures or open resources</strong> in constructor arguments. The job is stored as text, and a job that cannot be stored is refused when you dispatch it, with a message naming the class.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Reacting to a Permanent Failure</h3>
    <p>Implement <code>FailableJobInterface</code> to be told when a job has used up all its attempts. It runs once, after the job has been moved to the failed jobs.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\FailableJobInterface;
use Throwable;

final class ChargeInvoice implements FailableJobInterface
{
    public function __construct(public readonly int $invoiceId) {}

    public function handle(Billing $billing): void
    {
        $billing->charge($this->invoiceId);
    }

    public function failed(Throwable $exception): void
    {
        error_log("Invoice {$this->invoiceId} could not be charged: " . $exception->getMessage());
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p><code>failed()</code> is called directly, so it does <strong>not</strong> get container injection. If it throws, the error is logged and ignored.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Dispatching</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\QueueManager;

// 1. The global helper
dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($id));
dispatch(new SendReminder($id), queue: 'emails', delay: 3600);

// 2. Inject the manager (easier to test and to swap)
public function __construct(private QueueManager $queue) {}

$jobId = $this->queue->dispatch(new SendWelcomeEmail($id));
$waiting = $this->queue->size('emails');</code></pre>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
            <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Argument</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Meaning</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>$job</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">The job object to run.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>queue</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Name of the queue (1&ndash;100 characters). Default: <code>default</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>delay</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2">Seconds to wait before the job may first run. Default: <code>0</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>Both return the new job's id. A negative delay or an invalid queue name throws <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Retries</h2>
    <p>If <code>handle()</code> throws, the job is put back and tried again after a wait. When it runs out of attempts it moves to the <a href="#failed-jobs">failed jobs</a>. Tune this per job with attributes:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Backoff;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Timeout;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\Attributes\Tries;

#[Tries(5)]       // give up after 5 attempts
#[Backoff(30)]    // wait 30s, then 60s, 120s, 240s
#[Timeout(45)]    // abort an attempt that runs longer than 45s
final class ImportCatalog implements JobInterface { /* ... */ }</code></pre>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
            <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Attribute</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Default</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">What it does</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>#[Tries(int $times)]</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">3</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Total attempts, including the first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>#[Backoff(int $seconds, bool $exponential = true)]</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">10s, doubling</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Wait before a retry. With <code>exponential: false</code> the wait is always the same. Capped at one day.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>#[Timeout(int $seconds)]</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2">60s</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Longest an attempt may run. A job that exceeds it counts as a failed attempt. Needs the <code>pcntl</code> extension (see <a href="#good-to-know">Good to Know</a>).</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Running the Worker</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody queue:work                          # process the "default" queue
php rhapsody queue:work --queue=high,default     # "high" is always checked first
php rhapsody queue:work --stop-when-empty        # drain the queue, then exit</code></pre>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
            <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Option</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Default</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Meaning</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>--queue=a,b</code> (<code>-q</code>)</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>default</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Queues to process, in priority order. A later queue is only looked at when the earlier ones are empty.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>--sleep=N</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">3</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Seconds to wait when every queue is empty.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>--max-jobs=N</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">0 (no limit)</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Stop after this many jobs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>--max-time=N</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">0 (no limit)</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Stop after this many seconds. Checked between jobs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>--memory=MB</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">128</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Stop once memory use reaches this many megabytes, so a supervisor can start a fresh worker.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>--stop-when-empty</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2">off</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Exit as soon as every queue is empty. This is the mode for cron.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>On a Server You Control: Run It as a Daemon</h3>
    <p>Keep the worker running with a process manager such as Supervisor, which restarts it whenever it stops. The limit options let it recycle itself regularly.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-ini">[program:rhapsody-queue]
command=php /var/www/app/rhapsody queue:work --queue=high,default --max-time=3600
directory=/var/www/app
autostart=true
autorestart=true
numprocs=2
process_name=%(program_name)s_%(process_num)02d
stopwaitsecs=70</code></pre>
    <p>Run as many workers as you like: each job is claimed by exactly one of them. <code>stopwaitsecs</code> gives a worker time to finish its current job when the process manager stops it.</p>

    <h3>On Shared Hosting: Run It from Cron</h3>
    <p>No process manager? Start a worker every minute. It processes whatever is waiting and exits.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">* * * * * cd /path/to/app &amp;&amp; php rhapsody queue:work --stop-when-empty --max-time=55 &gt;&gt; /dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1</code></pre>
    <p>If a run is still busy when the next one starts, that is fine: the two simply share the work.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2 id="failed-jobs">Failed Jobs</h2>
    <p>A job that has used all its attempts, or whose stored data can no longer be read, is moved to the <code>failed_jobs</code> table together with the error and stack trace. Every failure is also written to <code>storage/logs/queue.log</code>.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody queue:failed          # list them
php rhapsody queue:retry 12        # put one back on its queue
php rhapsody queue:retry all       # put them all back
php rhapsody queue:flush           # delete them all (asks first; --force skips the question)</code></pre>
    <p>A retried job starts again with a fresh attempt count.</p>

    <h2>Commands</h2>
