                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>queue:install [--migrate]</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Publishes the migration for the <code>jobs</code> and <code>failed_jobs</code> tables. Safe to run twice.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>queue:work</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Processes jobs. See the options above.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>queue:failed</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Lists failed jobs with their id, queue, time and error.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>queue:retry &lt;id|all&gt;</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Puts one failed job, or all of them, back on their queue.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>queue:flush [--force]</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2">Permanently deletes every failed job.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to Know</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A job may run more than once.</strong> If a worker dies after finishing the work but before the job is marked done, the job is handed out again. Write jobs so that running twice does no harm (check whether the email was already sent, use an upsert instead of an insert).</li>
        <li><strong>Dispatch after you commit.</strong> Dispatch once the data the job needs is saved. A worker can pick the job up almost immediately, and it will not see changes that are still inside an open transaction.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep <code>#[Timeout]</code> below the retry window.</strong> A reservation that is older than 90 seconds is treated as abandoned and the job is handed to another worker. The default job timeout is 60 seconds, which fits; if you raise it, raise the driver's <code>retryAfter</code> too (see below).</li>
        <li><strong>Timeouts and a graceful stop need <code>pcntl</code>.</strong> On Linux and macOS the worker aborts a job that exceeds its timeout, and finishes its current job before exiting on <code>SIGTERM</code> or Ctrl+C. Windows (for example a local WAMP setup) has no <code>pcntl</code>: jobs run without a time limit, and stopping the worker mid-job leaves the job to be retried once its reservation expires.</li>
        <li><strong>A crashing job cannot loop forever.</strong> Every time a job is picked up it counts as an attempt. A job that keeps killing its worker still ends up in the failed jobs once its attempts are used.</li>
        <li><strong>Jobs are signed.</strong> Each job is stored with a signature made from your <code>APP_KEY</code>. A row that was altered, or inserted by hand, is rejected and moved to the failed jobs. If you rotate the key, list the old one in <code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> so jobs already waiting can still be read.</li>
        <li><strong>Renaming or deleting a job class</strong> makes waiting jobs of that class unreadable, and they go to the failed jobs. Let the queue drain first, or keep the old class until it has.</li>
        <li><strong>Restart workers after a deploy.</strong> A running worker keeps the old code in memory. Stop it (a process manager will start a fresh one) or let <code>--max-time</code> recycle it. Cron workers pick up new code on their own.</li>
        <li><strong>Queue order.</strong> Within a queue, jobs run oldest first. To make some work more urgent, give it its own queue and list that queue first: <code>--queue=high,default</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Works on every supported database.</strong> MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server and SQLite use the same tables and the same queries. The <code>priority</code> and <code>meta</code> columns are reserved for add-on modules and are not used by core.</li>
        <li><strong>If the tables are missing,</strong> the worker and <code>dispatch()</code> stop with a message telling you to run <code>queue:install --migrate</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Using a Different Database Connection</h2>
    <p>By default the queue uses the same database as the rest of your app. To point it somewhere else, or to change the retry window, bind your own <code>QueueInterface</code> in your project's <code>bootstrap.php</code>. The worker, <code>dispatch()</code> and every queue command pick it up automatically.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Bootstrap\ServiceRegistrar;
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\QueueInterface;
use Rhapsody\Core\Queue\DatabaseQueue;

return static function (ServiceRegistrar $services): void {
    $services->singleton(QueueInterface::class, fn () => new DatabaseQueue(
        static fn (): PDO => new PDO($dsn, $user, $password, [PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION]),
        retryAfter: 120,
    ));
};</code></pre>
    <p>The <code>jobs</code> and <code>failed_jobs</code> tables must exist in that database. <code>QueueInterface</code> is a small contract (<code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>release</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>fail</code>, plus the failed-job methods), so a Redis or SQS driver can be dropped in the same way.</p>

